# HTML injection: the announcement banner, and the CSS that finishes the PMO rebrand.
#
# Installed at: /home/admin/conf/web/pmo.gobrandfactory.com/nginx.ssl.conf_banner
#
# Everything textual — the tab title, meta tags, and every "Plane" in the UI — is
# rebranded in index.html and the built bundle themselves. See README, "bundle rewrite".
#
# This file does NOT rewrite <head>. It used to, and that was a mistake: React Router
# owns the head, so serving a title the bundle disagreed with desynchronised hydration
# and threw React #418/#423 on every page load. index.html and the bundle now say the
# same thing, and nginx stays out of it.
#
# The two inline-SVG marks (Plane's wordmark and its customer-logo strip) are handled by
# rules appended to the app's own stylesheet — see brand.css. They cannot be injected
# from here: React reconciles the document on hydration and removes injected nodes.
# The banner script survives only because it re-appends itself from a MutationObserver.

# In <head>, and deliberately NOT deferred.
#
# React reconciles the whole document on hydration and removes anything nginx injected.
# A deferred script only runs after parsing finishes, by which time React's async module
# script — already in cache — has often executed and torn the tag out, so the banner
# silently never ran. That race is why it appeared to work some loads and not others; a
# warm cache loses it almost every time.
#
# A plain synchronous script runs during parsing, before any module script exists. It
# costs one blocking fetch of ~10KB from the same origin. banner.js already waits for
# DOMContentLoaded before touching the DOM, and re-attaches itself from a MutationObserver
# afterwards, so React removing the tag later is harmless.
sub_filter '</head>' '<script src="/pmo-banner/banner.js"></script></head>';

sub_filter_once on;
